1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,570
Here is a function.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:04,890
e to the x, times sine x.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:07,280
Its first derivative takes

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:08,600
one use of the product rule.

5
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:10,250
The second takes two.

6
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,790
So what is its hundredth derivative?

7
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:15,060
Grinding through the product rule

8
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:17,290
a hundred times would fill pages.

9
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:19,460
But the answer fits on one line.

10
00:00:19,930 --> 00:00:21,030
It's the same function,

11
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,900
flipped upside down, and scaled

12
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:24,270
up by two to the fiftieth.

13
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:26,180
And you can see why.

14
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:28,520
Picture a flat plane.

15
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,030
One direction means e to the x cosine x.

16
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,590
The other means e to the x sine x.

17
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:36,170
Our function is an arrow in that plane.

18
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:38,280
Take its derivative, and the arrow

19
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,200
turns by an eighth of a turn, and

20
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:41,920
stretches by the square root of two.

21
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:43,480
Every derivative does

22
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:44,800
exactly the same thing.

23
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:47,500
A hundred eighth turns is twelve

24
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:49,580
and a half full turns, so the arrow

25
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,170
ends up pointing straight backwards.

26
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:52,940
And it has been stretched by

27
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:54,860
root two, a hundred times over.

28
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,630
That's two to the fiftieth.

29
00:00:57,630 --> 00:00:59,040
An operation that turns

30
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,400
arrows and stretches them.

31
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:02,000
That's not what calculus

32
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,020
is supposed to look like.

33
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:04,630
That's what a matrix does.

34
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:06,900
So here is the claim this

35
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:07,940
video is going to earn.

36
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:10,530
Taking a derivative is a linear map,

37
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:12,720
the same kind of move a matrix makes.

38
00:01:13,190 --> 00:01:15,140
Once you see it that way, a surprising

39
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:17,060
amount of calculus turns out to be linear

40
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:19,960
algebra, plus one extra idea: the limit.

41
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,520
The plus C you were told never

42
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,180
to forget is a null space.

43
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:26,520
Sine and cosine cycle forever

44
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:28,430
because the derivative is a rotation.

45
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,360
e to the x is special

46
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:31,790
because it is an eigenvector.

47
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,480
And the notes a guitar string

48
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,400
can play fall out of a matrix.

49
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,660
We'll start by turning a curve

50
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:39,660
into a list of numbers, and end

51
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:40,880
by listening to that string.

52
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:47,820
Take a curve, say a sine wave.

53
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:49,920
Forget the smooth line, and keep only

54
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,170
its height at eight evenly spaced points.

55
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,760
Eight numbers.

56
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:54,840
Stack them in a column, and you

57
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:56,590
have a vector in eight dimensions.

58
00:01:57,490 --> 00:01:59,140
Sample it at fifty points, and

59
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:00,680
the vector has fifty entries.

60
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:03,300
At a thousand, the column is so tall

61
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,230
that the dots blur back into a curve.

62
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:07,060
That's how a computer stores a

63
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:09,170
function: as a very long vector.

64
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:11,860
And functions behave like vectors.

65
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:13,800
Add two curves, and you add their

66
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:15,640
heights point by point, exactly

67
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,020
the way you add two columns.

68
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:19,000
Scale a curve by three, and

69
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:20,470
every entry scales by three.

70
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:22,140
Now watch what the

71
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:23,200
derivative does with that.

72
00:02:23,730 --> 00:02:25,020
The slope of a sum is

73
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:26,240
the sum of the slopes.

74
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:27,900
And the slope of three times a

75
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:29,770
curve is three times the slope.

76
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:32,320
Those two rules are the entire

77
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:34,240
definition of a linear map.

78
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:35,360
They are exactly the

79
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,100
rules every matrix obeys.

80
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,760
So if functions are vectors, and the

81
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:41,860
derivative respects adding and scaling,

82
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:43,880
the derivative should be a matrix.

83
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:44,970
Let's build it.

84
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:49,340
The slope at a point is roughly the rise

85
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:51,330
to the next point, divided by the step.

86
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,570
Next height, minus this height,

87
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:54,990
over the gap between them.

88
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:57,280
Now write that down for

89
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:58,480
every point at once.

90
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,560
Each row of the matrix looks at

91
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,400
two neighbors: minus one on the

92
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,320
diagonal, plus one just to its

93
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,960
right, and zeros everywhere else.

94
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,900
Then divide everything by the step.

95
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,780
Zoom out to a thousand points, and

96
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:14,233
the matrix has a million entries.

97
00:03:14,367 --> 00:03:15,670
Almost all of them are zero.

98
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:17,967
What's left is one thin,

99
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:19,400
bright band down the diagonal.

100
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:21,520
Now multiply.

101
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:23,320
Feed it the column for sine

102
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:24,870
x, and a new column comes out.

103
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:26,590
Plot it back as a curve,

104
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:27,880
and it's cosine x.

105
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:29,720
The computer just took a

106
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:31,640
derivative, and all it did was

107
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:33,400
multiply a matrix by a vector.

108
00:03:33,870 --> 00:03:35,720
Physics simulations and weather models

109
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,173
do calculus in exactly this spirit,

110
00:03:38,227 --> 00:03:41,293
with enormous, mostly empty matrices.

111
00:03:41,847 --> 00:03:43,900
That matrix is an approximation, and it

112
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,670
gets better as the points get closer.

113
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:47,820
But for some functions you don't

114
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,410
need an approximation at all.

115
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,930
Take the polynomials up to x cubed.

116
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:53,660
Every one of them is a mix

117
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:56,100
of four building blocks: one,

118
00:03:56,590 --> 00:03:58,140
x, x squared, and x cubed.

119
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:01,553
So a cubic is really just four numbers.

120
00:04:01,553 --> 00:04:03,290
The derivative sends one to zero,

121
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:05,830
x to one, x squared to two x,

122
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,060
and x cubed to three x squared.

123
00:04:08,610 --> 00:04:10,220
Write where each building block lands

124
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,220
as a column, and you get an exact

125
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:14,740
four by four matrix, with one, two,

126
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:16,810
three sitting just above the diagonal.

127
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,860
Multiply it by the four numbers

128
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:20,780
of any cubic, and out come the

129
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,220
four numbers of its derivative.

130
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:23,620
No limits.

131
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:24,590
No approximation.

132
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:27,770
Calculus, done entirely by a matrix.

133
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:31,450
Look at the first column of this matrix.

134
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:32,910
It's all zeros.

135
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:34,520
Feed it a constant, and

136
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:35,820
it returns nothing at all.

137
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,860
In the language of matrices, a whole

138
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,340
direction gets crushed to zero.

139
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:43,820
Every constant function, five, minus

140
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:46,370
two, a million, lands on the same point.

141
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:49,040
That direction is called the null space.

142
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:51,240
And a matrix that crushes a

143
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:53,090
direction can never be undone.

144
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:54,840
Its determinant is zero.

145
00:04:55,330 --> 00:04:56,880
Once two inputs land on the

146
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,720
same output, there is no way to

147
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:00,870
tell which one you started from.

148
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:02,480
x squared plus five, and x squared

149
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:05,220
minus two, both have derivative two x.

150
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:06,900
So think about what

151
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:08,530
integration really asks.

152
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,100
Find a function whose

153
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:11,440
derivative is this one.

154
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:13,300
That is solving a matrix

155
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:16,080
equation: D times F equals f.

156
00:05:16,490 --> 00:05:18,500
And linear algebra already knows what the

157
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:20,400
answer to a crushed system looks like.

158
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:22,483
One particular solution, plus

159
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:24,330
anything at all from the null space.

160
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,360
Here, the null space is the constants.

161
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,600
That's the plus C.

162
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:30,840
It isn't a rule to memorize, or

163
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:32,410
a way to lose marks on an exam.

164
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:33,960
It's the null space of the

165
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:36,040
derivative, showing up exactly

166
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:37,770
where linear algebra says it must.

167
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:40,910
This matrix has one more odd habit.

168
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:42,960
Each time you apply it, every

169
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:44,950
coefficient slides one slot up,

170
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:46,800
and the top one falls off the end.

171
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:48,540
Apply it four times,

172
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,050
and every cubic is gone.

173
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,140
Differentiate a polynomial enough

174
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:53,960
times, and nothing is left.

175
00:05:55,950 --> 00:05:57,933
Let's change the building blocks.

176
00:05:57,933 --> 00:05:59,800
Instead of powers of x, take just

177
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,930
two functions, cosine and sine.

178
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:03,960
Every mix of them, like two

179
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,280
cosine x plus sine x, is an arrow

180
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,280
in a plane, with cosine along one

181
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:10,030
axis and sine along the other.

182
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:12,850
Cosine's derivative is minus sine.

183
00:06:13,170 --> 00:06:14,690
Sine's derivative is cosine.

184
00:06:14,930 --> 00:06:16,460
So where does the cosine arrow

185
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:17,670
go when you differentiate it?

186
00:06:18,070 --> 00:06:19,110
Pause and predict.

187
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:24,540
Cosine, pointing right, swings

188
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:26,330
down to point at minus sine.

189
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:28,580
Sine, pointing up, swings

190
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:29,960
right to point at cosine.

191
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:31,780
Both arrows turn a quarter

192
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:32,990
turn, the same way.

193
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:34,420
On this plane, the

194
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:35,880
derivative is a rotation.

195
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:38,630
Now the old facts read differently.

196
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:40,440
Differentiate twice, and

197
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:41,960
every arrow has turned halfway

198
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:43,510
around and points backwards.

199
00:06:43,770 --> 00:06:45,240
That's why the second derivative

200
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:46,720
of sine is minus sine.

201
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Four derivatives make a full turn and

202
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:51,840
bring you home, which is why sine, cosine,

203
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:54,540
minus sine, minus cosine cycle forever.

204
00:06:54,830 --> 00:06:56,440
It's the same quarter turn that

205
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,320
makes i squared equal minus one.

206
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:01,430
And this is the secret behind the opening.

207
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,580
Swap in e to the x cosine

208
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:05,520
x, and e to the x sine x.

209
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:07,980
Now each derivative turns by an eighth

210
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:10,160
of a turn, and stretches by root two.

211
00:07:10,530 --> 00:07:12,300
Unlike the polynomial matrix,

212
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:13,750
this one crushes nothing.

213
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:15,553
Its determinant is two,

214
00:07:15,607 --> 00:07:16,520
so it can be undone.

215
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:18,400
That gives a trick you

216
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:19,210
might not have seen.

217
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:21,200
The integral of e to the x sine

218
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:23,040
x usually takes integration by

219
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,300
parts, twice, and a bit of algebra.

220
00:07:25,580 --> 00:07:27,280
Here, it is just the inverse

221
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,200
matrix: turn back by an eighth of

222
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:31,040
a turn, and shrink by root two.

223
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:33,480
Out comes one half e to the x,

224
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,470
times sine x minus cosine x.

225
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:37,360
Differentiate that, and you get

226
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:39,110
e to the x sine x right back.

227
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:40,960
Add the plus C, and you're done.

228
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:45,020
In linear algebra, the most revealing

229
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:47,230
question you can ask a matrix is this.

230
00:07:47,850 --> 00:07:49,700
Which directions does it leave alone?

231
00:07:50,190 --> 00:07:51,500
Which vectors does it only

232
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:53,010
stretch, and never turn?

233
00:07:53,990 --> 00:07:55,660
So ask the derivative.

234
00:07:55,930 --> 00:07:57,360
Which function comes back

235
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:58,720
from differentiation as

236
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,240
just a multiple of itself?

237
00:08:00,830 --> 00:08:01,970
Pause and predict.

238
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:06,390
Not sine.

239
00:08:06,740 --> 00:08:08,580
The derivative turns it into cosine.

240
00:08:08,990 --> 00:08:10,070
Not x squared.

241
00:08:10,330 --> 00:08:11,370
It loses a power.

242
00:08:11,790 --> 00:08:13,830
The answer is e to the x.

243
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:15,640
Its derivative is itself.

244
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:18,410
And e to the two x comes back doubled.

245
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:21,560
e to the minus x comes back flipped.

246
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:23,320
Every function e to the lambda

247
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:24,880
x is an eigenvector of the

248
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,110
derivative, with eigenvalue lambda.

249
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:29,500
That's the deepest reason

250
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:30,980
e shows up everywhere.

251
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:32,700
Whenever something changes at

252
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:34,500
a rate proportional to itself,

253
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:36,700
money, populations, radioactive

254
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:38,620
atoms, it is asking for an

255
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:40,110
eigenvector of the derivative.

256
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:42,900
And only one family of functions answers.

257
00:08:43,970 --> 00:08:45,040
Differential equations

258
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:46,440
become linear algebra too.

259
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:49,040
Take y double prime plus y equals

260
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,440
zero, the equation of a spring.

261
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:53,040
Read it as an operator: D

262
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:56,053
squared plus one sends y to zero.

263
00:08:56,053 --> 00:08:57,600
So its solutions are exactly

264
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:59,180
the null space of that operator.

265
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:02,320
A null space is always flat, and this one

266
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:05,190
is a plane: every mix of cosine and sine.

267
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:07,973
Two numbers pick out one solution,

268
00:09:08,027 --> 00:09:09,200
and that's why a spring needs

269
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:10,880
two starting conditions, where it

270
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,280
begins and how fast it's moving.

271
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:14,320
It also explains a trick

272
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:15,490
from earlier on this channel.

273
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:18,240
The Fourier and Laplace transforms rewrite

274
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:20,210
a function as a mix of exponentials.

275
00:09:20,450 --> 00:09:22,320
In that basis, the derivative's

276
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:24,480
own eigenbasis, differentiating

277
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:26,120
stops being calculus.

278
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:27,120
Each piece just gets

279
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:29,200
multiplied by its own number.

280
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:30,640
That is what turning calculus

281
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:32,290
into algebra really means.

282
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:34,410
It's diagonalizing the derivative.

283
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,500
Let's put the matrix to work

284
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:38,660
on something you can hear.

285
00:09:39,540 --> 00:09:42,213
A guitar string is pinned at both ends.

286
00:09:42,267 --> 00:09:43,547
Where it bends more sharply,

287
00:09:43,573 --> 00:09:44,780
it gets pulled back harder.

288
00:09:45,050 --> 00:09:46,720
And how sharply a curve bends

289
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:47,810
is its second derivative.

290
00:09:48,670 --> 00:09:49,940
So build the second

291
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:51,350
derivative as a matrix.

292
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,390
Two hundred points along the string.

293
00:09:53,630 --> 00:09:55,887
Each row takes a point's two neighbors,

294
00:09:55,913 --> 00:09:57,730
and subtracts twice the point itself.

295
00:09:58,140 --> 00:09:59,840
One, minus two, one.

296
00:10:00,210 --> 00:10:01,890
Pin both ends at zero.

297
00:10:02,870 --> 00:10:04,600
Now ask the computer the question

298
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:06,340
linear algebra loves most.

299
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:08,750
What are this matrix's eigenvectors?

300
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:10,670
Which shapes does it only scale?

301
00:10:11,770 --> 00:10:12,800
Out come these.

302
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:14,080
A single arch.

303
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:15,110
Two arches.

304
00:10:15,370 --> 00:10:15,710
Three.

305
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:18,540
Smooth sine waves, each fitting a whole

306
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:20,450
number of half waves between the pins.

307
00:10:20,690 --> 00:10:22,700
Nobody typed in a sine.

308
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:23,820
They fell out of a grid

309
00:10:23,900 --> 00:10:25,360
of ones and minus twos.

310
00:10:25,590 --> 00:10:27,020
And their eigenvalues match

311
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:29,020
the exact answer, minus k

312
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:31,100
pi squared, to three digits.

313
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:33,520
Each eigenvector is a way the string

314
00:10:33,640 --> 00:10:35,520
can vibrate in place, keeping its

315
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:37,640
shape and only growing and shrinking.

316
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:40,040
Its eigenvalue sets how fast.

317
00:10:40,310 --> 00:10:42,400
The second shape vibrates twice as

318
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,320
fast as the first, one octave up.

319
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:46,570
The third, three times as fast.

320
00:10:47,630 --> 00:10:49,140
Pluck the string, and its shape

321
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:50,780
is a mix of these eigenvectors,

322
00:10:51,090 --> 00:10:52,670
each ringing at its own speed.

323
00:10:53,250 --> 00:10:56,127
That mix is the sound of the note.

324
00:10:56,127 --> 00:10:57,300
The overtones you hear are

325
00:10:57,380 --> 00:10:58,960
the eigenvectors of a matrix.

326
00:11:00,930 --> 00:11:01,850
One last move.

327
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:05,087
For numbers, e to the x is one, plus

328
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:08,720
x, plus x squared over two, and so on.

329
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:10,530
That same series works for matrices.

330
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:12,400
So what happens if you put the

331
00:11:12,480 --> 00:11:14,270
derivative itself in the exponent?

332
00:11:15,450 --> 00:11:18,500
e to the h D is one, plus h times the

333
00:11:18,580 --> 00:11:21,060
derivative, plus h squared over two times

334
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:23,860
the second derivative, and on and on.

335
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:25,300
Apply it to a function, and you

336
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,060
get f, plus h f prime, plus h

337
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:29,870
squared over two f double prime.

338
00:11:30,410 --> 00:11:31,710
That's the Taylor series.

339
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:33,540
And the Taylor series adds up

340
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:35,990
to f, evaluated at x plus h.

341
00:11:37,090 --> 00:11:38,440
So the exponential of the

342
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:39,800
derivative is a shift.

343
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,740
It slides the whole graph sideways by h.

344
00:11:43,290 --> 00:11:45,000
Tiny changes, added up,

345
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,400
become one big move.

346
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:48,800
One honest asterisk.

347
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,340
This only works for functions whose Taylor

348
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:53,380
series actually adds back up to them,

349
00:11:53,420 --> 00:11:56,540
like polynomials, e to the x, and sine.

350
00:11:56,620 --> 00:11:58,670
There are smooth functions where it fails.

351
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:01,020
e to the minus one over x squared is

352
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:03,180
perfectly smooth, yet every one of

353
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:05,450
its derivatives at zero is zero, so

354
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:07,470
its Taylor series there is just zero.

355
00:12:09,410 --> 00:12:12,030
So, is calculus secretly linear algebra?

356
00:12:12,510 --> 00:12:13,690
Mostly, yes.

357
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:16,787
But the honest answer has a second half.

358
00:12:17,287 --> 00:12:18,940
A real function has a value at

359
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:20,690
every point, not just a thousand.

360
00:12:20,910 --> 00:12:23,020
It's a vector with infinitely many

361
00:12:23,100 --> 00:12:25,670
entries, and infinity changes the rules.

362
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,220
The derivative is no longer a finite

363
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:30,120
matrix, and it has no determinant.

364
00:12:30,410 --> 00:12:32,660
On the space of all smooth functions,

365
00:12:32,970 --> 00:12:35,180
every number is an eigenvalue, because

366
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:38,167
e to the lambda x works for any lambda.

367
00:12:38,167 --> 00:12:39,500
And getting from a thousand points

368
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,150
to every point takes a limit.

369
00:12:41,730 --> 00:12:42,700
That's the part linear

370
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:44,040
algebra can't do for you.

371
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,880
Making all of this rigorous is a field

372
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:49,020
of its own, called functional analysis.

373
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:50,960
It's the mathematics underneath

374
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,640
quantum mechanics, where every

375
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:54,040
quantity you can measure is an

376
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:55,540
operator, just like the derivative.

377
00:12:56,680 --> 00:12:57,730
But the picture holds.

378
00:12:58,180 --> 00:12:59,840
Plus C is a null space.

379
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:02,390
Sine and cosine are a rotation.

380
00:13:02,710 --> 00:13:04,550
e to the x is an eigenvector.

381
00:13:04,710 --> 00:13:07,490
A guitar string is an eigenvector problem.

382
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:11,300
Calculus is linear algebra, plus a limit.
